   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to the penalty for a moving violation which results in the serious bodily injury or death of another person",
    "basePrintNo": "S6748",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6748-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 57,
       "fullName": "Catharine Young",
       "imgName": "414_catharine_young.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 414,
       "sessionMemberId": 855,
       "sessionYear": 2015,
       "shortName": "YOUNG"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1800, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6748\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to the\npenalty for a moving violation which results in the serious bodily\ninjury or death of another person\n\n SUMMARY OF BILL :\n\nThis legislation will provide a minimum financial penalty for persons\nwho commit a moving violation (violation of Totle 7 in the Vehicle and\nTraffic Law), should such violation result in death or serious bodily\ninjury.\n\nSection 1: Creates a new paragraph I of Section 1800 of the Vehicle\nand traffic Law.\n\nSection 2: Sets the enactment date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCurrently, there is no minimum financial penalty for committing a\nmoving violation in vehicle and traffic law, that results in the death\nor serious bodily injury - unless such moving violation is considered\nVehicular Manslaughter - the violation of serious vehicle and traffic\nlaws; 1E, driving under the influence or with a revoked/suspended\nlicense. In these serious scenarios, the result is a felony charge.\n\nWe have heard from police officers and parents of victims across the\nstate who are dealing with the death of a loved-one as a result of\nanother's driving infractions that do not raise to the level of a\nVehicular Manslaughter charge.\n\nRecently in Monroe County, a driver was charged with violating section\n1142 of the vehicle and traffic law - failure to yield the right of\nway - when failing to stop at a yield sign when pulling onto a main\nroad, while striking and killing an oncoming motorcyclist. The driver\nof the car was only charged with a $30 fine for failing to yield the\nright of way. We believe that these less serious offenses should\nresult in larger minimum penalties, should they result in the death of\nanother.\n\nOther States currently set minimum and maximum fines and punishments\nfor moving violations that causes serious impairment of a body\nfunction or death of another person for multiple scenarios - IE.\nMisconduct with a motor vehicle, criminal negligence in the operation\nof a motor vehicle, negligent homicide with a motor vehicle, different\ndegrees of vehicular manslaughter, etc.\n\nOur neighbors in Connecticut have different layers of laws relating to\nmanslaughter with a motor vehicle. The most relatable offense that CT\nhas on the books, which relates to this proposal, is Negligent\nHomicide with a Motor Vehicle (CGS Section 14-222a).\n\nIn Connecticut a person is guilty of negligent homicide with a motor\nvehicle when, because of his negligent operation of a motor vehicle,\nhe causes someone's death. The penalty is up to six months in jail, a\n$1,000 fine, or both. This offense involves ordinary negligence which\noccurs when someone deviates from the standard of care or conduct a\nperson should reasonably follow under the circumstances. This is\neasier to prove than misconduct with a motor vehicle which involves a\ngross deviation from the standard of conduct that should be followed.\n\nIn Pennsylvania, unintentionally killings such as committing a moving\nviolation would be categorized as Involuntary manslaughter.\nInvoluntary manslaughter is a first degree misdemeanor, with potential\npunishment of a term of imprisonment for up to five year - and if the\ndefendant committed involuntary manslaughters as the parent,\ncaregiver, or custodian of a child under the age of twelve, state law\nincreases the criminal charge to a second degree felony.\n\nThis legislation simply looks to create a minimum penalty for the\nviolators of vehicle and traffic law whose actions result in the death\nor serious bodily injury of another - similar to our neighboring\nstates.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect 60 days after enactment.\n",
